= Appendix B - Interview Questions

*Section 1: General Use & Student Requirements*

How often do you currently use social or academic platforms to connect with other students?

What are some challenges you face while trying to engage with your university community online?

In your opinion, what is missing from existing platforms that JULink could address?

*Section 2: Account & Security*

How important to you does email verification (authentication by OTP) come in the areas of trust and security?

What are the password recovery or account security features you expect?

How does it make you feel to have the authority to deactivate or permanently remove your account?

*Section 3: Posting & Interaction*

When making posts, would you choose plain text, images, or a combination? Why?

How would post titles and tags be helpful in structuring and searching for content?

What kind of interactions (likes, comments, shares) do you find most engaging in a student platform?

How will the system deal with posts and comments that are not appropriate or are considered offensive?

*Section 4: Feed & Content Personalization*

Would you like to have most of the posts in your feed come from your college, or a combination of other universities too?

How much does it matter to you to filter or curate your feed (e.g., by college, by interest tags, or by popularity)?

How does it make you feel to view ads or sponsored posts in your news-feed?

*Section 5: Profile & Personalization*

What parts of your profile (e.g., photograph, school/major, college) are most significant for you to manage and edit?

Would you like to display your academic interests or extracurricular activities on your profile?

How much do you value being able to retrieve and go back to your previous posts, comments, and liked posts?

*Section 6: Ads & Monetization*

How acceptable to you would it be to experience ads on JULink if it would make the service available at no charge?

What kind of ads would you find most relevant or least disrupting in an academic environment?

How probable would you be to click on an advertisement if it has relevance to student needs (e.g., books, courses, events)?

What should the platform provide to let you report inappropriate or harmful ads?

*Section 7: Expectations & Future Improvements*

What would encourage you to use JU Link daily instead of other platforms? 

Which one do you believe would be most important to help strengthen the university community?

What issues (usability, privacy, performance) would make you refrain from utilizing JULink?

What would you suggest as one area for improvement or new feature for JULink?
